<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman Republic</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman Republic was the system of government of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the expulsion of the kings, traditionally dated to 509 BCE, until the rise of Augustus in the late first century BCE. Governed by elected magistrates and the Senate, it expanded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Hellenistic kingdoms. It laid the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> note, overcame the Macedonian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-republic.jpg" alt="Roman Republic" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_republic,_territory_44_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Roman Republic</b> destroyed Carthage at the end of the Third Punic War in 146 BCE, it turned the city's remaining territory in what is now northern Tunisia into the province of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory of Etruscan civilization reached its greatest extent around 500 BCE, shortly after the Roman Kingdom became the <b>Roman Republic</b>, and the decline began in the first half of the 5th century BCE with the loss of the southern provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Pontus mattered most as the base of Mithridates VI, whose wars against Rome lasted, with intervals, from 89 to 63 BCE and shaped the politics of the late <b>Roman Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Numidia, united by Masinissa after the Second Punic War, was the first great Berber kingdom, spreading Punic urban culture inland, helping Rome destroy Carthage and then, under Jugurtha, fighting a war that exposed the corruption of the <b>Roman Republic</b> before its absorption into Roman Africa in 46 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…annexed Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, Ferrara and Romagna at the Treaty of Tolentino in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a <b>Roman Republic</b> and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Empire emerged from the ruins of the <b>Roman Republic</b>, itself the product of centuries of expansion from a small city-state on the banks of the Tiber in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
